@startuml
' Feature toggle creation and consumption flow

title Feature Toggle Flow (Kubernetes + HTTP PUT Callback Per Pod)

actor "Product/Dev Team" as Product
participant "Server Service\n(Feature Toggle API)" as Server
database "Toggle Database" as DB
participant "Client Pod\n(Spring Service Instance)" as Consumer
participant "Feature Toggle Library\n(Spring Starter)" as Lib
collections "Local Toggle Cache" as Cache

== Feature toggle creation ==
Product -> Server: Create toggle\n(name, service, enabled, value?, syncStatus)
Server -> DB: Persist toggle
DB --> Server: OK
Server --> Product: Creation confirmation

note over Product,Server
MVP: value is optional as a child object.
When provided: value.type = STRING|NUMBER and value.raw.
end note

note over Server,DB
Each toggle has syncStatus per registered instance.
Examples: SYNCED, PENDING_RESPONSE, OUT_OF_SYNC.
end note

== Declaration in the Spring application ==
note over Consumer
application.yml / application.properties
declares consumed toggles with one strategy per key
ex: feature.toggles.consumed.new-checkout=LOCAL_CACHE
ex: feature.toggles.consumed.payment-v2=REMOTE_ALWAYS
defines the callback path for updates through PUT
ex: feature.toggles.callback-url=/internal/feature-toggles
end note

note over Server,Lib
MVP: the server does not decide or validate the strategy per toggle.
The strategy is the responsibility of the client library/application.
end note

note over Consumer,Lib
In Kubernetes, each pod runs this startup independently
and registers as a distinct instance.
end note

== Application startup registration ==
Consumer -> Lib: Spring context initialization
Lib -> Lib: Read toggles declared in the application
Lib -> Server: POST /clients/register\n(serviceName, instanceId, podName, namespace,\nconsumed toggles list, callbackUrl)
Server -> DB: Validate and fetch current values
DB --> Server: Toggle values
Server --> Lib: Response with toggle map\n(enabled, value?)
Lib -> Cache: Store toggles in memory
Lib --> Consumer: Registration completed

note over Server,DB
The server controls registration, version, and syncStatus by instanceId.
Each pod must have its own callbackUrl; the Service address does not guarantee
delivery to every replica.
end note

== Runtime toggle usage (strategy per toggle) ==
Consumer -> Lib: isEnabled("new-checkout")
Lib -> Lib: Apply the local strategy defined by the client

alt Strategy = LOCAL_CACHE
  Lib -> Cache: Query local value
  Cache --> Lib: true/false
  Lib --> Consumer: true/false
else Strategy = REMOTE_ALWAYS
  Lib -> Server: GET /toggles/new-checkout?service=consumer-service
  Server -> DB: Fetch current toggle value
  DB --> Server: Current value
  Server --> Lib: true/false
  Lib --> Consumer: true/false
end

alt Toggle enabled
  Consumer -> Consumer: Run the new flow
else Toggle disabled
  Consumer -> Consumer: Keep the current flow
end

note over Consumer,Lib
Goal: reduce server calls on every toggle usage by bootstrapping on startup
and allowing a per-toggle strategy: LOCAL_CACHE or REMOTE_ALWAYS.
end note

== Toggle change and propagation to LOCAL_CACHE ==
Product -> Server: Change toggle value (ex: new-checkout=false)
Server -> DB: Persist the new toggle value
DB --> Server: OK
Server -> Server: Identify registered pods that consume the toggle

loop For each registered pod consuming the toggle
  Server -> DB: Mark instance syncStatus as PENDING_RESPONSE
  Server -> Consumer: PUT {callbackUrl}/new-checkout\n(enabled, value?, version, updatedAt, instanceId)
  Consumer -> Lib: Forward received update
  Lib -> Lib: Apply local strategy (ex: update cache if LOCAL_CACHE)
  Consumer --> Server: HTTP 200 OK (standard PUT response)
  Server -> DB: Update instance syncStatus to SYNCED
end

alt HTTP response not received before timeout
  Server -> DB: Mark instance syncStatus as OUT_OF_SYNC
  Server -> Server: Schedule delivery retry
end

note over Server,Consumer
In this model, the server performs an HTTP PUT callback
to the endpoint exposed by the client.
The HTTP 200 OK response represents standard delivery confirmation.
This lets LOCAL_CACHE pods avoid querying the server on every isEnabled(),
while staying up to date.
Each replica is handled individually.
New pods receive the current snapshot during bootstrap.
end note

note over Consumer
MVP: strategy implementation is required in the client.
The server only delivers values and records delivery state.
end note

== Instance lifecycle in the cluster ==
Consumer -> Lib: Pod shutdown / application termination
Lib -> Server: DELETE /clients/register/{instanceId}
Server -> DB: Remove instance registration
DB --> Server: OK
Server --> Lib: Confirmation

alt Pod terminates without deregister
  Server -> Server: Expire registration by heartbeat timeout
  Server -> DB: Mark instance as inactive
end

@enduml
